PCI: size bridge windows for child alignment padding

pbus_size_mem() accumulates the required window size with

	size += max(r_size, align);

which is correct only when a child's size is a multiple of its alignment.
Every child must *start* at an aligned address, so a child whose size is not
a multiple of its alignment leaves a gap before the next one. That gap is
never budgeted, and the resulting window can be too small to place all the
children even though the arithmetic says it fits.

Example: four GPUs each with a 64GB BAR1 (64GB alignment) plus a 32MB BAR3,
so each downstream port window is 64GB+32MB with 64GB alignment. Summing
sizes without padding reserved 256GB; with alignment each child occupies
128GB and four need 512GB. Two GPUs can silently get no BAR1 even though
the host bridge still has free MMIO.

Using ALIGN() instead of max() budgets the inter-child padding. The two are
identical whenever r_size <= align, which covers every ordinary device BAR
(where size == alignment), so this only changes the bridge windows that are
currently mis-sized.

--- a/drivers/pci/setup-bus.c	2026-07-17 12:24:12.000000000 -0700
+++ b/drivers/pci/setup-bus.c	2026-08-07 18:45:50.198049365 -0700
@@ -1331,7 +1331,16 @@
 				continue;
 
 			r_size = resource_size(r);
-			size += max(r_size, align);
+
+			/*
+			 * Every child must start at an aligned address, so a
+			 * child whose size is not a multiple of its alignment
+			 * leaves a gap before the next one. Round up so that
+			 * padding is included; this is identical to
+			 * max(r_size, align) whenever r_size <= align, which
+			 * covers ordinary device BARs where size == alignment.
+			 */
+			size += ALIGN(r_size, align);
 
 			/*
 			 * If resource's size is larger than its alignment,
